9. Parking permits and unpaid tickets
Question: What must I do before parking in a campus Student Parking lot, and what happens if I leave a parking ticket unpaid for more than 30 days?
Expected answer: Register your vehicle with Security and purchase a parking permit. After 30 days, an unpaid ticket’s fine doubles and is added to your student account.
Source document ID(s): handbook-vehicle-parking
Expected evidence: “Students must register their vehicles and purchase a parking permit in order to park in on campus Student Parking lots.” “Tickets must be paid within 30 days and may be paid in the Security Office. After 30 days, the fine will double and the charge will be added to the student account.”
Answerable: Yes.

10. Accounting holds while living on campus
Question: I am already living on campus for summer housing or an activity. When must I clear an Accounting/Enrollment hold, and what happens if I do not?
Expected answer: Clear the hold no later than one week before classes begin. Otherwise, you must move out and cannot attend classes or participate in co-curricular activities. Student Accounts may provide advance written permission to remain under an approved action plan.
Source document ID(s): handbook-account-holds
Expected evidence: “Students who are already living in the Residence Halls due to summer housing or co-curricular activities must clear an Accounting/Enrollment hold no later than one week before the first day of classes.” “Students who do not meet this deadline will be required to move out of the Residence Halls and may not attend classes or participate in co-curricular activities.” “In some cases, the Student Accounts Office may provide written authorization for a student to live or remain in campus housing while following an approved action plan.”
Answerable: Yes.

11. Repeating a course
Question: I earned a D+ in a course. Can I repeat it, and which attempt counts toward my GPA and earned units?
Expected answer: A D+ is eligible for repetition under the policy, subject to its exceptions, faculty determination, and available space. The most recent attempt counts toward GPA and earned units. The previous attempt receives zero credit and its grade is excluded from GPA calculation.
Source document ID(s): catalog-grading-policy
Expected evidence: “Students may repeat a course if they received a grade of ‘D+’ or lower.” “Credit for the grade from the most recent course attempt will be used in calculating the grade point average and earned unit count. Credit for the previous course attempt will be set to zero; the previous grade will not be used in determining the grade point average.” “Enrollment in a repeated course is subject to availability of and space in the course, which are not guaranteed.”
Answerable: Yes.

12. Eligibility for an incomplete
Question: Under what circumstances can I receive an incomplete grade?
Expected answer: An incomplete may be granted for serious illness or injury, a family crisis, or another documented unforeseen circumstance beyond your control that prevents completion. It is intended for a limited amount of remaining work, including qualifying circumstances near the end of term or earlier circumstances followed by unsuccessful good-faith efforts to catch up. Simply missing assignments or having many absences does not qualify.
Source document ID(s): catalog-grading-policy
Expected evidence: “Incomplete grades shall be granted only in cases of serious illness or injury, family crisis, or some other substantiated unforeseen circumstance beyond the control of the student that would make it impossible to complete all course requirements by the end of the semester.” “A student may not receive an incomplete for a course because of failure to complete required assignments on time and/or because of a significant number of absences occurring during the course of the term.”
Answerable: Yes.

13. Dropping a course after the first week
Question: How do I drop a course after the first week of classes but before the drop deadline?
Expected answer: Obtain an Add/Drop Card from the Registrar’s Office, provide the course details, obtain written approval from the instructor and primary academic advisor, and submit the completed card to the Registrar.
Source document ID(s): registrar-registration
Expected evidence: “A student can drop a course after the first week of classes and before the relevant drop deadline by acquiring an Add/Drop Card from the Registrar’s Office on the second floor of Pearsons Hall.” “The Registrar’s Office requires the drop details and written approval of the relevant course instructor(s) and primary academic advisor (AMP or Major), via an Add/Drop Card to process a drop request.”
Answerable: Yes.

14. Graduation credit and GPA requirements
Question: How many earned units do I need to graduate, and what minimum GPA is required overall and in my major?
Expected answer: You need at least 31 earned units, a GPA of at least 2.000 in all Beloit courses attempted, and a GPA of at least 2.000 in courses in your major. These are part of the graduation requirements, not the complete list.
Source document ID(s): registrar-graduation-requirements
Expected evidence: “Completion of a departmental or interdisciplinary major, with a cumulative grade point average of at least C (2.000) in all courses in the major.” “Completion of at least 31 units of earned credit, with a cumulative grade point average of at least C (2.000) in all Beloit courses attempted.”
Answerable: Yes.

